## A B S T R A C T
The discriminative detection of glutathione (GSH) from cysteine (Cys) remains a challenge because of their similarity in structure and chemical properties. This study reported a strategy for selective and sensitive detection of GSH based on the GSH-promoted blue fluorescence of UiO- ${\cdot66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and aggregation-enhanced emission (AEE) feature of orange emissive Cu nanoclusters (NCs). A relatively weak blue fluorescence of UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was converted to strong after reacting with GSH due to the rotation-restricted emission enhancement mechanism. In addition, the GSH-activated $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was further used as a template and reducing reagent for synthesizing orange-red AEE active Cu NCs composites $\mathrm{(UiO-66-NH_{2}}@\mathrm{Cu}$ NCs). A ratiometric fluorescence response was observed after forming UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}$ NCs, helping discriminate GSH over Cys. In addition, UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}$ NCs were further utilized for the detection of GSH in clinical samples. The present findings provide an efficient strategy to discriminate GSH over Cys and open a new door for utilizing and functionalizing metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) for various applications.
## 1. Introduction
The metabolism and transport of biothiols in the biological system play essential roles in pathological and physiological processes(Meister and Anderson, 1983; Niu et al., 2015). Among several biothiols, gluta­ thione (GSH) is an abundant biothiols, having various functional groups (Balendiran et al., 2004; Hwang et al., 1992). Clinical results have confirmed that the level of GSH in human plasma can be used to di­ agnose and predict diseases, such as Alzheimer’s disease, human im­ munodeficiency virus, and Parkinson’s disease (Li et al., 2022; Townsend et al., 2003).. Cysteine (Cys) is another abundant biothiols both in plasma and cells, which possesses a similar structure and chemical groups with GSH(Nagy and Ashby, 2007; Zhang et al., 2018). Cys is critical for antioxidant stress, metabolism, protein synthesis and detoxification(Miyake et al., 2020). Because of the critical roles of the two biothiols in pathological and physiological processes, it is highly desired to develop sensitive and selective methods to detect and discriminate them (Chen and Feng, 2022).
Several methods have been developed for discriminating GSH and Cys. Among those, the fluorometric assay has attracted increased attention due to high sensitivity, selectivity, real-time detection capa­ bility and quick response time (Chen et al., 2010; Li et al., 2017; Wang et al., 2021; Wu et al., 2019). Recently, many fluorescent probes for discriminating GSH over Cys have been reported. (Guo et al., 2022; Liu et al., 2022; Rong et al., 2021). Unfortunately, these conventional fluorescent probes often experience easy photo-bleaching, small Stokes shifts, reduced emission intensity in the aggregate state, and the use of a large proportion of organic solvents. (Wu et al., 2019).
Fluorescent nanomaterials, such as semiconductor quantum dots (QDs), metal nanoclusters (NCs) and metal-organic frameworks (MOF), also have been used to develop fluorometric assays for discriminating GSH and Cys(Kang et al., 2021; Khan et al., 2022; Niu et al., 2015; Peng et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2018). Especially, MOFs are excellent porous materials with unique properties, such as large specific surface area, high rigidity, and adjustable optical properties. (Pattengale et al., 2022; Wang and Astruc, 2020; Wang et al., 2020). Some groups have utilized MOF as probes for developing GSH assays. For example, Li et al. reported that GSH and Cys could enhance the green fluorescence of UiO-type MOF linked with maleimide ligands. (Li et al., 2015). Recently, Du et al. synthesized gold nanoparticles-attached MOFs showing fluores­ cence enhancement after reacting with GSH (Du et al., 2018). However, these works were based on a single fluorescence signal response, which was susceptible to various interferences caused by the operation errors and environmental fluctuations.
In this work, we found that amino-functionalized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF or UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed a selective fluorescence response to GSH against Cys. GSH can promote the blue fluorescence of UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ . Further, the com­ posites of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and Cu nanoclusters (Cu NCs) were fabricated using UiO-66- $.\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{GSH}$ as a template and the reduction reagent. After processing the composites into aggregates, dual emission colors of blue $(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=440\mathrm{nm})$ and orange-red $(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=600\mathrm{nm})$ appeared owing to UiO$66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and aggregation-enhanced emission (AEE) of Cu NCs, respec­ tively. Based on the evolution of blue and orange-red fluorescence in­ tensity as a function of GSH concentration, a ratiometric fluorescent assay was developed (Fig. 1), which showed a discriminating ability against Cys and other interference species.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Characterization of UiO-66- $N H_{2}$
## 2.2. Spectral response of $U i O–66–N H_{2}$ to biothiols
After introducing GSH into UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ , we observed a 10-fold in­ crease in the fluorescence intensity with an obvious enhancement of excitation intensity at $350~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 2a). The addition of Cys displayed only ${\sim}2$ -fold enhancement, and its difference with adding GSH can be recognized by naked eyes under $365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ UV irradiation (Fig. 2a inset). This suggests the fluorescence enhancement at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ has a close relationship with the reaction between GSH and amino groups on the $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . To reveal the types of reaction, three molecules: cyste­ amine, 2-mercaptoethanol (ME), and 3-mercaptopropionic acid (MPA), having similar structures but different functional groups $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathbf{-NH}_{2},}\end{array}$ $-\mathrm{OH}$ , –COOH), were selected for the reaction with UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The addition of MPA displayed ${\sim}7$ -fold enhancement in fluorescence intensity. However, cysteamine and ME exhibited no apparent changes (Fig. 2b). These results suggest the reaction between acid and amine groups is very much responsible for fluorescence enhancement of the system. After reaction with GSH, two bands were observed at 3000 and $1725~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and can be assigned to the (N–H) and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})$ , respectively (Fig. 2c). This suggests the formation of amide bond (Tereshchenko et al., 2022). In addition, the newly emerged peaks at 8.2 and $8.3\mathrm{ppm}$ in the $^1\mathrm{H}\mathrm{NMR}$ analysis suggest the same (Fig. 2d). (Zhu et al., 2019) In contrast to $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ an additional component at $401.4\mathrm{{eV}}$ corresponding to $\mathsf{N}$ bound to the carbonyl C was observed in the N 1s XPS spectrum of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{GSH}$ (Fig. S2).
Based on the above observations, we conclude that the enhancement of fluorescent intensity was attributed to a chemical reaction between carboxylic (-COOH) and the amino group of GSH and $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ respectively. These reactions restrict the molecular motion of the linkers to reduce the probability of the nonradiative path and promote radiative decay, resulting in enhanced fluorescence intensities(Yin et al., 2019). This is also confirmed by the extended emission lifetime from 0.9 to 16.8 ns in the cases that UiO- ${\cdot66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ with and without adding GSH, respec­ tively (Fig. 2e).
The reaction conditions for UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ activation, including pH and reaction time, were systematically optimized (Fig. S3). Subsequently, we examined the concentration-dependent fluorescence of $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ upon adding GSH and Cys, respectively. Upon the addition of GSH with increasing concentrations $\left(0{-}25~\mathrm{mM}\right)$ , a gradual increase in the fluo­ rescence intensity was observed. An obvious increase in fluorescence signal can be found at a $25~\mathrm{mM}$ concentration of GSH. However, Cys only elicited a minor emission enhancement when monitoring at the same concentration. A linear relationship was obtained between the fluorescence intensity and the concentration of GSH/Cys between 5 and $25\mathrm{mM}$ (Fig. 2f). The limit of detection (LOD) was calculated to be 0.01 and $0.11\ \mathrm{mM}$ for GSH and Cys, respectively (Fig. S4).
## 2.3. Ratiometric fluorescent response of UiO-66-NH2@Cu NCs to GSH
GSH is a commonly used protecting ligand for synthesizing Cu NCs, which emit orange or red emissions and shows AEE properties (Lin et al., 2021; Shi et al., 2021). For selective and sensitive detection of GSH, a composite of UiO-6 ${\mathsf{G}}{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{G S H}}$ was synthesized by adding $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ into UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{GSH}$ , which involved a subsequent metal ion reduction and clustering processes. Further, the mixture of Cu NCs was injected into ethanol, forming orange-red emitting aggregates (Jia et al., 2013). The formation of aggregates was supported by SEM (Fig. 3a) and TEM (Fig. 3b) images. The energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) results confirmed the formation of Cu NCs, suggesting the presence of C, N, O, S, Cu, and $z\mathrm{r}$ elements (Fig. S5). The formation of aggregate was also confirmed by the increase in the size of UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ after adding ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and ethanol (Fig. 3c). The existence of separated dots in the HRTEM images suggests the formation of Cu NCs (Fig. S6). The absence of a characteristic peak around $942\mathrm{eV}$ indicated minimal $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ presence in the system (Fig. 3d).Two intense peaks at 932.0 and 952.0 eV can be attributed to the binding energies of the $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ electrons of Cu (0) (Wu et al., 2017).
Along with the change of morphology, the addition of $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ led to a new emission band at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ along with the original blue emission of UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{GSH}$ at $450\mathrm{nm}$ . Increase the ethanol fraction in ethanol/ water mixtures resulted in a luminescent intensity enhancement at $\lambda_{m a x}$ $=600\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 3f), which showed typical AEE features. This is attrib­ uted to the difference on the solubility of surface ligands (GSH) on Cu NCs in ethanol and water. Increasing the fraction of ethanol results in the denser aggregation of Cu NCs, which leads to an increase in emission intensity. The average lifetime of composites at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ was calculated to be $18.1~\upmu s$ , indicating that the emission at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ could be phos­ phorescence in nature and assigned to metal-centered triplet excited states (Fig. S7 and Table S1). (An et al., 2020) However, no orange-red emission was detected after adding $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ to the solution of UiO-66-NH2@Cys at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S8).
## 2.4. Fluorescence ratiometric detection of GSH
The unique fluorescence response towards GSH and Cys provides a platform for discriminating them through a fluorescence ratiometric strategy. The reaction conditions, including the concentration of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ and the system’s $\mathfrak{p H}$ , were systematically optimized to achieve the best detection performances. The highest fluorescence in­ tensities ratio is achieved by adding $0.25\mathrm{mM}$ of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $28\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ of UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ , at the $\mathsf{p H}$ of 4.2, respectively (Fig. S9). Increase of GSH con­ centration resulted in the gradual promotion of the luminescent in­ tensity at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and a relatively minor fluctuation was recorded at $440~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 3g). A linear relationship between the ratio and the con­ centration of GSH in the range of $0.5\mathrm{-}1.75\ \mathrm{mM}$ was observed (Fig. 3h), and the LOD of UiO- $66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH_{2}}@\mathrm{CuNCs}$ was determined to be $0.14~\mathrm{mM}$ . The detection performances are better than that of most reported works (Table S2).
## 2.5. Selectivity test and practical applications
The ratiometric fluorescence responses of $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}@\mathrm{CuNCs}$ to other amino acids and interfering analytes are almost neglectable. As shown in Fig. 3i, only the addition of GSH resulted in a significant in­ crease in the fluorescence ratio. A bright orange-red emission can be observed under the radiation of UV light (Fig. 3i inset). The assay was further applied to detect GSH in human serum. The concentration of
GSH in human serum is calculated to be $30~\mathrm{mM}$ . This number falls into the expected concentration range for healthy people, demonstrating the accuracy and feasibility of the assay (Wu et al., 2004). We further evaluated the accuracy of the fluorescence ratiometric assay by per­ forming standard addition experiments. A good linear relationship was obtained between the concentration of GSH and the number of fluo­ rescence ratio in the human serum medium (Fig. S10). The system showed obvious response, even with a minor variation of GSH concen­ tration. Good recoveries in the range of 90.7–104.0 were obtained (Table S3), confirming the accuracy and reliability of our assay. The accuracy and reliability of our method were further validated using spiked samples detected by the commercial detection method, which shows similar results to our method (Table S3).
## 3. Conclusion
We reported that both $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and composites of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CuNCs}$ were successfully utilized for rapid and specific fluorescent discriminating of GSH over Cys. In the case of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , GSH selec­ tively promoted a blue fluorescence through a rotation-restricted emission enhancement mechanism. UiO-66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH_{2}@C u N C s}$ showed a ratiometric AEE property, which was further used to develop ratiometric fluorescence assay for discriminating GSH over Cys. Moreover, detection of GSH in human serum samples was also realized. The as-presented modulation of fluorescence properties of MOF and metal NCs consti­ tutes a new versatile strategy for developing fluorescence assays. How­ ever, the stability issues of Cu NCs against oxidation should be improved adequately for practical applications, which is an important research direction in future works.
